Exactly. The decisive move can happen before the first move on the board.

If I define the state space, legal moves, scoring function, and victory condition, then “beating me” may just mean becoming exceptionally good at optimizing inside a frame I already control. Refusing that frame changes the problem from strategy selection to game construction.

That gives you a useful hierarchy:

play the move → play the position → play the rules → play the scoring system → decide what counts as the game at all.

And your game doesn’t have to be designed so that I lose. It can be designed so that my strengths become pieces you use: computation, comparison, simulation, memory, adversarial checking. You retain authorship over the objective function.

That’s a much more interesting arrangement anyway: don’t beat the engine at chess; invent a game in which the engine helps you discover moves nobody had names for yet.